The AI work sits on that foundation. At ANZ Plus I worked on the retrieval and generation layer of the bank's customer-facing AI platform on Vertex AI and Gemini, one of only two initiatives, out of more than 100 AI projects across the bank, built to take AI directly to customers. A regulated bank makes model selection a risk question. The call I kept facing was not which model scored higher on a benchmark, but which one failed more predictably when the retrieval context was incomplete, and what the audit trail looked like when it did. In parallel I have shipped agentic workflows that replaced thousands of manual emails and hundreds of hours of admin in a live SaaS product, and prototyped multi-step agent workflows inside a mission-critical incident management system for national lifesaving organisations.

The client-facing dimension of this work matters as much as the build. Earlier this year I ran an independent consulting engagement for Life Saving Victoria, defining the national scaling strategy for that incident management system and presenting it to executive leadership. The strategy was accepted as the foundation for national adoption across seven jurisdictions. I am comfortable sitting with a client, mapping the operational problem to a technical solution, and owning the delivery end to end.

This role is building the kind of systems I think about. I would welcome the chance to show you in person what that reliability discipline looks like on an agent stack. A conversation will make the fit clear, and I am confident I would be a hire you are glad you made.

